{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And behold, the plague is dim, and the plague has not spread</b> Rashi wrote (Rashi on Leviticus 13:6): \"dim\" — that it has dimmed from its appearance; hence if it remained in its appearance and did not spread — it is impure. This is indeed the plain meaning of the verse, but according to the midrash of our Sages it is not so, for we learned (Negaim 1:3): he confines the one who stands [unchanged] at the end of the first week and releases the one who stands [unchanged] at the end of the second week. And in explanation they said in Torat Kohanim (Negaim 3:8): in the case of garments, one that stands [unchanged] in the first week is confined and in the second is burned; but in the case of a person, one that stands [unchanged] in the first week is confined and in the second they release him. And they further said in Megillah (8a): a quarantined metzora is excluded whose tzara'at is not dependent on his body but on the days. And had it been required that it be dim, his purification would have been dependent on his body. And Rashi himself explained there in this language, that it is not dependent on his body — that if no sign of impurity, white hair or spreading, is found on the seventh day, he declares him pure even though his plague stands unchanged before his eyes, such as at the end of the second week. But thus the Sages said: at the end of the second week, whether the plague dimmed from the appearance of snow to [the appearance of] the lime of the Sanctuary, or whether it resembles the membrane of an egg, and even the bright spot that was like lime and returned to snow, and all the more so if it stood in its appearance — anything that did not spread — he declares it pure. If so, the meaning of the verse \"and behold, the plague is dim\" is that it reverted to the appearance of a different plague, such as from snow to lime; since it did not spread — it is a mipaḥat, so that you should not say: since the plague changed to the appearance of a different plague, it should be considered as appearing anew [at the start]; the verse comes and teaches that it is pure. And the same applies if it intensified, for the verse has already taught you that a change from one appearance to another is not a sign of impurity, but rather it is as though it stands unchanged, and anything that did not spread is pure. And if you ask: why did the verse not mention intensification, and all the more so dimming — it comes to teach you that even if it dimmed, if it spread it is impure. And the meaning of \"dim\" is that it dimmed to one of the appearances of plague-afflictions, such as from snow to the membrane of an egg, which is still a plague; but below [i.e., lighter than] the appearances of plague-afflictions, it has already healed and there is no plague there, and spreading in such a case does not render impure at all. And in this manner it is explained in Torat Kohanim (here)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:53:32.760206824Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}